WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.320
Imagine two identical computers,
copying the exact same 8-megabyte file.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.000
One finishes instantly. The other
creeps along, taking several seconds.

3
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:19.310
We are looking at a gap of less than a
millisecond compared to seconds of agonizing

4
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.660
delay. Both programs use
the exact same logic.

5
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.010
The only altered variable is the size of
the memory buffer they use to hold the

6
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:33.410
data. How does changing the size of a
single buffer dictate the speed of a basic

7
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:38.590
input and output task? To find the answer,
we have to look closely at how a process

8
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.240
actually communicates with
the operating system.

9
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.670
Every process is born with three open file
descriptors, zero for standard input, one

10
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.740
for standard output, and
two for standard error.

11
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:53.830
The running program is blind to what
these numbers actually represent.

12
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.160
It merely uses the integers
to request or send data.

13
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.790
It is the shell that assigns meaning to
these descriptors using operators like less

14
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.260
than or greater than. The shell maps
them to a file, a terminal, or a pipe.

15
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.200
This output table from the course bench
tool shows the real performance cost.

16
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:17.530
Notice the row using a 64-kilobyte buffer
completes the task in 129 calls, while the

17
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.100
one-byte buffer row forces over eight
million individual calls to the system.

18
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:25.840
The physical storage drive is
exactly the same in both tests.

19
00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.350
Because the file descriptor completely
abstracts the destination, the hardware isn't

20
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.650
slowing us down. The massive delay is caused
directly by the sheer volume of those

21
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.120
system calls. Think about
the read system call.

22
00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.450
The program asks the kernel for data and
the call returns the exact number of bytes

23
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.120
it successfully retrieved.

24
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.390
The rules are strict. If the call reaches
the end of a file, it returns exactly

25
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.940
zero. If an error occurs,
it returns negative one.

26
00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.200
But there is a major caveat
in systems programming.

27
00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.700
A read call might return fewer bytes
than your program originally requested.

28
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:01.820
This partial read happens frequently.

29
00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:06.410
The program might be pulling data from
a network socket, reading a pipe, or the

30
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.740
process might be interrupted by a signal.

31
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.310
Because the actual payload size is
unpredictable, developers can never trust a

32
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.920
single read call to fetch an entire file.

33
00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.760
The write system call suffers
from the exact same limitation.

34
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.300
It's equally vulnerable
to partial transfers.

35
00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:26.550
A write can easily be cut short if the
receiving pipe or socket is completely full,

36
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:30.060
or if a signal interrupts the
process before the data leaves.

37
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.460
This code shows the
mandatory C countermeasure.

38
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.680
A while loop repeatedly calls write
until all requested bytes are pushed out.

39
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:42.390
The lab enforces this, placing read inside
an infinite loop for retries, building

40
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.060
robust wrappers like
read_exact and write_all.

41
00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.810
Ensuring robust input and output forces
programs into continuous looping.

42
00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.500
That looping directly multiplies
the number of system calls executed.

43
00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.190
Every time your program calls read and write,
the CPU must physically halt execution

44
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:03.600
in user mode. It then jumps across the
privilege boundary down to kernel mode.

45
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.700
This mandatory round trip
is a context switch.

46
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.470
Saving the registers and validating the
arguments incurs an unavoidable time

47
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.320
penalty. On the course machine,
we measured this exact toll.

48
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.340
It costs roughly 0.7 microseconds for
every single read and write iteration.

49
00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:24.390
The operating system charges this exact
same 0.7 microsecond toll whether the call

50
00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.500
transports a massive payload
or just a single byte.

51
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.070
The true bottleneck is never
the volume of data being moved.

52
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.520
It is the volume of boundary
crossings required to move it.

53
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.550
We can now return to our opening eight
megabyte mystery and apply this toll math to

54
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.280
the one-byte buffer scenario.

55
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.910
Moving eight megabytes, one byte at a time,
forces the program to cross the boundary

56
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.720
8.4 million times. That is why it
takes several seconds to finish.

57
00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.730
Contrast this with the 64-kilobyte buffer.

58
00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:59.530
It transports the exact same eight megabytes
of data but it requires only 129 total

59
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:05.670
iterations. Because the program only pays
the context switch toll 129 times, the

60
00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.180
total operation time drops
to less than a millisecond.

61
00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.120
This gives us the golden
rule of system architecture.

62
00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.320
Never operate one byte
at a time in a loop.

63
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.770
Always aggregate your data into buffers
of a few kilobytes or more to minimize

64
00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.280
kernel trips. You do not always
have to do this manually.

65
00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.630
Standard C libraries apply this buffering
rule automatically under the hood when

66
00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.740
utilizing functions like printf or fwrite.

67
00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.430
Let's review. Question one, what does a
read call return when it reaches the end of

68
00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.140
a file? It returns zero.

69
00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.780
Question two, why does copying a file
one byte at a time take so long?

70
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:51.920
Because every system call pays a fixed
time toll to cross the privilege boundary.

71
00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:59.360
Now open animations A2.3 and A2.4
in the course hub and run lab TD1.
